From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: 0xalpharush <0xalpharush@protonmail.com>
Date: Sun, 26 Oct 2026 00:00:01 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [compiler-rt] sealed operator new: run the new_handler
 contract, not a single throw
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

🤖: Patch 0023 made a sealed throwing operator new call std::__throw_bad_alloc()
after one failed asan_memalign.  That is not what the program's own operator new
does.  libc++abi's and libstdc++'s operator new is a loop: on a null allocation
it loads the current handler with std::get_new_handler(); if there is one it
calls it -- the handler may free memory and return (the allocation is then
retried) or it may throw -- and only when there is NO handler does it throw
std::bad_alloc.  The nothrow forms call that throwing form inside a try/catch
and return null on any exception.

By throwing on the first failure, 0023's sealed binary diverges from its plain
twin exactly when a new_handler is installed: the handler's side effects never
run, memory it would have freed is never retried into, a handler that throws its
own exception type has that type replaced by bad_alloc, and a nothrow new
returns null where the handler would have let it succeed.  Audited: a handler
that records a call and then throws is invoked once on the plain build (calls=1)
and zero times on the sealed build (calls=0, exit 12).  Each is a difference the
sanitizer made, which is what the sealed/plain equivalence is supposed to
exclude.

This replaces SealedThrowBadAlloc with SealedNewLoop, which runs the contract.
Seal off: it returns null and the caller keeps upstream behaviour
(ReportOutOfMemory for a throwing form, null for a nothrow form) -- unsealed
builds are byte-for-byte unchanged in behaviour.  Seal on: it loops --
get_new_handler(); no handler -> throw bad_alloc (throwing) or return null
(nothrow); a handler -> call it and retry asan_memalign.  std::get_new_handler
is hand-declared beside std::__throw_bad_alloc for the reason 0023 gives (the
runtime pulls in no C++ header; the symbol is the unversioned
_ZSt15get_new_handlerv), and the strong reference extracts libc++.a's
definition the same way.  A handler that throws unwinds through this frame as
__throw_bad_alloc already does (-funwind-tables; the _Unwind_RaiseException
interceptor unpoisons the abandoned stack).

One residual is left, and flagged, because it is the substantial part of the
full fix: a nothrow operator new must turn an exception the handler throws into
a null return, and the catch that does so needs this translation unit compiled
with exceptions, which compiler-rt builds -fno-exceptions.  The catch is written
below under ASAN_SEALED_NEW_HANDLER_EXCEPTIONS, which defaults to 0 so the stock
build is unchanged; with it off, a nothrow new whose installed handler THROWS is
the one case still unmatched (the exception escapes the noexcept operator new
and terminates, where a plain binary catches and returns null).  A handler that
frees and returns -- the common case -- is matched in both builds.  Enabling the
catch means adding -fexceptions for this single file in compiler-rt's CMake.
The maintainers may prefer the narrower alternative of documenting 0023's
plain/sealed equivalence as holding only for programs with no installed
new_handler; this patch takes the broader route and leaves that choice visible.
---
 compiler-rt/lib/asan/asan_new_delete.cpp | 166 +++++++++++++++++++-------
 1 file changed, 102 insertions(+), 64 deletions(-)

--- a/compiler-rt/lib/asan/asan_new_delete.cpp
+++ b/compiler-rt/lib/asan/asan_new_delete.cpp
@@ -64,87 +64,125 @@
 // versioned", so the symbol is _ZSt17__throw_bad_allocv with no __1 in it,
 // and libstdc++ defines the same name; both as an exported, out-of-line,
 // noreturn function whose body is `throw bad_alloc()`.  Not weak, on purpose:
-// SealedThrowBadAlloc below says why the reference has to be strong.
+// SealedNewLoop below says why the reference has to be strong.
 [[noreturn]] void __throw_bad_alloc();
+// libc++/libstdc++'s accessor for the current new_handler, hand-declared for
+// the same reason: the runtime includes no C++ header.  Both libraries put it
+// in the unversioned namespace std, so the symbol is _ZSt15get_new_handlerv;
+// new_handler is the standard void(*)() handler type.  Its member is extracted
+// by this same strong reference.
+using new_handler = void (*)();
+new_handler get_new_handler() noexcept;
 #endif
 }  // namespace std
 
-// What a sealed binary's operator new does when the allocation is refused:
-// throws std::bad_alloc, as the program's own operator new would have.
+// How a sealed binary's operator new answers a refused allocation: it runs the
+// std::operator new contract its plain twin runs, rather than throwing on the
+// first failure.
 //
 // The rule is the one patch 0022 applies at the primary allocator's commit:
 // the sanitizer's own failures are unreachable; the program's may-fail
-// outcomes stay visible.  For malloc that outcome is a
-// NULL, and under allocator_may_return_null the allocator hands it up as one.
-// For a throwing form of operator new it is std::bad_alloc: libc++abi's
-// operator new (libcxxabi/src/stdlib_new_delete.cpp) loops on malloc and
-// throws when it returns null with no new_handler installed, and a program
-// written `try { p = new T[n]; } catch (const std::bad_alloc &)` goes on from
-// the catch.  This file replaces that operator new -- eight weak definitions
-// under the real mangled names, in libclang_rt.asan_cxx.a, which only a C++
-// link pulls -- and upstream's replacement answers the refusal with
-// ReportOutOfMemory whatever allocator_may_return_null says: the flag makes
-// asan_memalign return the null, and the null is then reported here.  In a
-// sealed binary that is the sanitizer dying where the program's plain build
-// catches and continues -- a difference the sanitizer made rather than one
-// it found.
-// So when the seal is on and a throwing form has a null in hand, the runtime
-// throws what the program's operator new would have, and ReportOutOfMemory
-// is not reached.  The nothrow forms keep returning the null, which is their
-// contract.  Unsealed behaviour is unchanged.  A refusal the allocator itself
-// reports -- allocator_may_return_null off, or a size over the maximum -- is
-// what it was, for new as for malloc.
+// outcomes stay visible.  For a throwing form of operator new the program's
+// outcome is not simply std::bad_alloc.  libc++abi's and libstdc++'s operator
+// new (e.g. libcxxabi/src/stdlib_new_delete.cpp) is a loop: on a null
+// allocation it loads the current handler with std::get_new_handler(); if
+// there is one it calls it -- the handler may free memory and return, in which
+// case the allocation is retried, or it may throw -- and only when there is no
+// handler does it throw std::bad_alloc.  The nothrow forms call that throwing
+// form inside a try/catch and turn any exception into a null return.
 //
-// How a runtime compiled with -fno-exceptions throws: it does not; it calls
-// libc++'s std::__throw_bad_alloc(), which does, and the exception unwinds
-// through this frame on its way to the program's handler.  That needs the
-// frame's CFI and nothing more, since nothing here has a destructor to run,
-// and compiler-rt compiles with -funwind-tables (SANITIZER_COMMON_CFLAGS), so
-// the .eh_frame entry is there.  ASan's own part of a throw --
-// __asan_handle_no_return, unpoisoning the stack the exception abandons --
-// runs from the _Unwind_RaiseException interceptor, which __cxa_throw calls
-// and the wrap list attaches (libunwind.a is in the intersection the list is
-// cut to; __cxa_throw, libc++abi's, is not, so its own interceptor is not).
+// Patch 0023 replaced all of that with a single std::__throw_bad_alloc() after
+// one failed asan_memalign.  A sealed binary then skipped the handler entirely:
+// a handler's side effects never ran, memory it would have freed was never
+// retried into, a handler that throws its own type had that type replaced by
+// bad_alloc, and a nothrow new returned null where the handler would have made
+// it succeed.  Each is a difference the sanitizer made, not one it found --
+// the very thing the sealed/plain equivalence forbids.
 //
-// Why the declaration is strong and compiled only into the statically
-// intercepting runtime.  Nothing else in a static C++ link need name
-// _ZSt17__throw_bad_allocv -- libc++abi's operator new throws directly, and
-// is replaced by this file anyway -- so the archive member that defines it,
-// libc++.a's new_helpers.cpp.o, is extracted because this object refers to
-// it; a weak reference extracts nothing, and the call would go to address 0.
-// The cost is that a C++ link against this runtime has to provide a C++
-// library, which every C++ link does, and one that does not fails on this
-// symbol at link time rather than later.  C links never see asan_cxx.a.  The
-// reference lives where the runtime's other link-time expectation does,
-// __asan_wrap_list_present of patch 0002: in the runtime whose interceptors
-// the link binds, and not in a shared runtime, which a C program may load.
-static void SealedThrowBadAlloc() {
+// SealedNewLoop runs the contract.  When the seal is off it returns null at
+// once and the caller keeps upstream's behaviour (ReportOutOfMemory for a
+// throwing form, null for a nothrow one).  When the seal is on it loops:
+// get_new_handler(); no handler -> throw bad_alloc (throwing) or return null
+// (nothrow); a handler -> call it and retry asan_memalign.  A handler that
+// throws unwinds through this frame the same way __throw_bad_alloc does, which
+// 0023 already established works (-funwind-tables, the _Unwind_RaiseException
+// interceptor unpoisons the abandoned stack).
+//
+// Residual, flagged for review: the nothrow forms must turn an exception the
+// handler throws into a null return, and a catch needs this TU compiled with
+// exceptions -- compiler-rt builds it -fno-exceptions.  The catch is written
+// below under ASAN_SEALED_NEW_HANDLER_EXCEPTIONS, off by default so the stock
+// build is unchanged; with it off, a nothrow new whose installed handler
+// *throws* is the one case still unmatched (it escapes a noexcept function and
+// terminates, where a plain binary returns null).  A handler that frees and
+// returns -- the usual case -- is matched in both builds.  Turning the macro
+// on means adding -fexceptions for this one file in compiler-rt's CMake; see
+// the PR description.  Why the std references are strong and compiled only into
+// the statically intercepting runtime is unchanged from patch 0023: nothing
+// else in a static C++ link names them, so the strong reference is what
+// extracts libc++.a's definitions; a C link never pulls asan_cxx.a.
+#ifndef ASAN_SEALED_NEW_HANDLER_EXCEPTIONS
+#define ASAN_SEALED_NEW_HANDLER_EXCEPTIONS 0
+#endif
+static void *SealedNewLoop(uptr align, uptr size, BufferedStackTrace *stack,
+                           AllocType type, bool nothrow) {
 #if SANITIZER_STATIC_INTERCEPTION
-  if (SealedInit())
-    std::__throw_bad_alloc();
+  if (!SealedInit())
+    return nullptr;
+  for (;;) {
+    std::new_handler nh = std::get_new_handler();
+    if (!nh) {
+      if (nothrow)
+        return nullptr;
+      std::__throw_bad_alloc();
+    }
+#  if ASAN_SEALED_NEW_HANDLER_EXCEPTIONS
+    if (nothrow) {
+      try {
+        nh();
+      } catch (...) {
+        return nullptr;
+      }
+    } else {
+      nh();
+    }
+#  else
+    nh();
+#  endif
+    void *res = asan_memalign(align, size, stack, type);
+    if (LIKELY(res != nullptr))
+      return res;
+  }
+#else
+  (void)align;
+  (void)size;
+  (void)stack;
+  (void)type;
+  (void)nothrow;
+  return nullptr;
 #endif
 }
 
 // TODO(alekseyshl): throw std::bad_alloc instead of dying on OOM.
-// A sealed binary does, through SealedThrowBadAlloc above; the unsealed
-// runtime keeps dying.
+// A sealed binary runs the new_handler contract through SealedNewLoop above;
+// the unsealed runtime keeps dying.
 // For local pool allocation, align to SHADOW_GRANULARITY to match asan
 // allocator behavior.
-#define OPERATOR_NEW_BODY(type, nothrow)            \
-  GET_STACK_TRACE_MALLOC;                           \
-  void *res = asan_memalign(0, size, &stack, type); \
-  if (!nothrow && UNLIKELY(!res)) {                 \
-    SealedThrowBadAlloc();                          \
-    ReportOutOfMemory(size, &stack);                \
-  }                                                 \
+#define OPERATOR_NEW_BODY(type, nothrow)                   \
+  GET_STACK_TRACE_MALLOC;                                  \
+  void *res = asan_memalign(0, size, &stack, type);        \
+  if (UNLIKELY(!res))                                      \
+    res = SealedNewLoop(0, size, &stack, type, nothrow);   \
+  if (!nothrow && UNLIKELY(!res))                          \
+    ReportOutOfMemory(size, &stack);                       \
   return res;
-#define OPERATOR_NEW_BODY_ALIGN(type, nothrow)                \
-  GET_STACK_TRACE_MALLOC;                                     \
-  void *res = asan_memalign((uptr)align, size, &stack, type); \
-  if (!nothrow && UNLIKELY(!res)) {                           \
-    SealedThrowBadAlloc();                                    \
-    ReportOutOfMemory(size, &stack);                          \
-  }                                                           \
+#define OPERATOR_NEW_BODY_ALIGN(type, nothrow)                     \
+  GET_STACK_TRACE_MALLOC;                                          \
+  void *res = asan_memalign((uptr)align, size, &stack, type);      \
+  if (UNLIKELY(!res))                                              \
+    res = SealedNewLoop((uptr)align, size, &stack, type, nothrow); \
+  if (!nothrow && UNLIKELY(!res))                                  \
+    ReportOutOfMemory(size, &stack);                               \
   return res;
 
